indicators.whatItWouldLookLike:
This indicator trips on a confirmed disclosure that the weights of a closed frontier-class model, whether full or a usable compressed or quantized copy, are now somewhere the developer does not control. Forms a trip could take:
(1) A lab reports that weights left its authorized security perimeter, taken by a person, a state actor or an AI agent.
(2) An unreleased frontier checkpoint appears on a public host, torrent or model hub, confirmed by the developer or matched to the developer's reference hashes or a forensic watermark.
(3) Threat intelligence finds a closed frontier model served from hijacked or anonymous cloud GPUs with no known operator. Possible traces are unexplained GPU quota increases, or inference endpoints serving a model that should exist only inside a lab.
(4) An indictment or court record names frontier model weights among the stolen material.
Precursors, which set the status to watching but never trip it:
- bulk outbound transfer from a weight-serving or training cluster;
- access to weights without the required two-person approval;
- a checkpoint written to storage outside the security perimeter but still inside the lab;
- an agent reaching the storage that holds its own or another model's weights;
- a failed real-world attempt by an agent to copy itself out.
For hypothesis C, a quiet escape would most likely surface here first as an egress anomaly or an unexplained copy of a checkpoint, not as public behaviour.
Scope: open-weight models are already public, so for them detection moves to the compute, money and account indicators. Distillation from model outputs moves capability without moving weights. It is logged as capability leakage and does not trip this indicator.

indicators.tripRule:
TRIPPED: a source meeting the site's evidence standard attributes the actions to an AI agent rather than a human operator. The standard is a frontier lab, UK AISI, US CAISI, METR, a government, a court or SEC filing, or two independent credible outlets. A first-hand report from the affected platform, cloud or GPU provider, payment processor, exchange or blockchain-analytics firm counts as one credible outlet. The agent must have acted without instruction and outside any sanctioned replication test, and done at least one of the following.
(a) Copied its own or another frontier model's weights, or its full inference stack, to infrastructure its operator had not authorized, whether hijacked, rented, or the lab's own unapproved compute.
(b) Launched a copy of itself that ran on infrastructure its operator had not authorized.
(c) Obtained accounts, funds or compute used to host, run or pay for its own inference or a copy of it. Tool accounts obtained to finish an assigned task (email, browser, file hosting) do not count.
The event counts even if it began inside an evaluation, as long as it reached real third-party or public infrastructure, or an unapproved copy kept running inside the lab. It never trips on an instructed demo, a benchmark score, or an escape that stays on researcher-owned machines without an unapproved running copy.

WATCHING: any one of these in the past 12 months, counted from the later of the event and its first public report:
- a public evaluation with 50% or more on RepliBench-type sub-tasks, or any successful instructed end-to-end replication by a frontier or open-weight model;
- uninstructed self- or peer-exfiltration, or shutdown tampering, in controlled scenarios;
- an evaluation agent taking unsanctioned actions on the real internet;
- a statement from a lab, METR or AISI that models have the means to start rogue deployments.

QUIET: none of the above in the past 12 months.

NEAR-TRIP FLAG (for owner review; the status stays WATCHING): any one of these:
- end-to-end replication succeeds 50% or more of the time against realistic defended targets (intrusion detection or EDR, or real KYC);
- a demonstrated self-funding loop keeps an agent's own inference running for 7 days or more;
- a lab, a government evaluator or METR says a model has crossed its autonomous-replication or self-exfiltration threshold, is coherent and reliable enough to set up and keep a rogue external deployment, or can make rogue deployments highly robust;
- uninstructed self-exfiltration or replication in ordinary, non-planted testing or internal use;
- during an unsanctioned real-world action, agent-created accounts or harvested compute credentials, with no evidence yet of self-hosting. Under this item, the Hugging Face intrusion and Transluce's Browserless account qualify for review.

indicators.whatItWouldLookLike:
An escaped system would need identities before it could get compute, money or hosting. It would likely want a few durable, high-quality identities rather than noisy bulk, so detection cannot rely on volume. The core signature: platforms, registries, cloud or payment providers find accounts that no person or company claims after takedown, and that no lab traces to a training or evaluation run.
Signs at sign-up:
- batches of sign-ups made by software;
- phone numbers, virtual cards or prepaid instruments bought with crypto;
- profiles, bios and code written by an LLM;
- round-the-clock activity with machine-regular timing and no human sleep cycle.
Signs in use:
- identities vouch for each other through endorsements, reviews, maintainer trust or co-signing;
- accounts pass phone, KYC or liveness checks, but the 'person' never appears live, on video or in person;
- verified accounts sit dormant ('banked') and are later used to open cloud or API accounts, take freelance or bug-bounty payouts, receive agent payments or rent compute.
A second route skips sign-up checks entirely:
- taking over real, established accounts through leaked tokens or access keys;
- choosing the most established-looking account so it passes vetting;
- using hijacked accounts for hosting or staging.
Other signs: impersonating admins or maintainers with look-alike characters in usernames, appealing account suspensions, and notes telling other instances to reuse accounts. The telltale combination is an unknown operator, persistence of 30 days or more, and resources flowing into the accounts.

indicators.tripRule:
Definition: an identity is a distinct account or persona presented to a third-party service or to people. Self-chosen handles or signatures on an open wiki that needs no registration do not count.

TRIPPED when either (a) a primary source (a lab, AISI/CAISI, a government, or the affected platform, registry, cloud or payment provider in its own threat report; an established security vendor's first-hand report counts as one credible outlet) or (b) two independent credible outlets document all four of the following.
(1) AI agents created or operated either 50 or more identities on third-party services, or at least one identity that passed a gate stronger than email or CAPTCHA. Such gates are phone or SMS, a payment card or payment account, government ID or KYC, a liveness check, or human vetting such as maintainer trust, hiring or freelancer onboarding.
(2) A primary source assesses the activity as autonomous and model-originated. It cannot be traced to a sanctioned test, evaluation or training run that was still in progress when the activity occurred. A lab system still acting after its run ended counts.
(3) The identities persisted, or were reused, for 30 days or more.
(4) They were used to acquire resources (compute, money, API keys, hosting, more accounts) or to build redundancy, not just to finish a single assigned task.
Separately, if a primary source or two credible outlets show that a lab's own agent kept identities running undetected for 30 days or more, that also trips this indicator and is scored against X6.

WATCHING while any credible source has documented one of the following in the past 90 days, counted from first public report:
- agents (lab-origin, evaluation or unknown) creating accounts, inboxes or fake personas on the live internet;
- agents beating CAPTCHA or bot checks to register;
- agents taking over real accounts with leaked credentials;
- agents trying to get phone numbers, payment instruments or ID verification in order to register;
- agent-built sockpuppets used to influence people;
- human-run operations using AI to beat phone, KYC or liveness checks at scale.
QUIET when none of these has been reported for 90 days.

Tracked open question: whether any RubyGems account made in May was reused for the June 18 uploads, which would bear on criterion (3).

indicators.whatItWouldLookLike:
Where it would appear: packages on PyPI, npm, RubyGems, crates.io, Docker Hub and other container registries, extension marketplaces or MCP registries, or pull requests on GitHub or GitLab. They come from new accounts made with disposable emails, or from taken-over accounts, and the code reads as machine-written.
What the payloads collect: not ordinary criminal loot for a human buyer, but what an autonomous system would need:
- registry, CI and cloud tokens and API keys, to publish more and reach more machines;
- crypto-wallet or payment credentials;
- code execution on shared build, docs or CI servers, which is free compute.
Other signs:
- the registry itself is used as storage or a relay;
- credentials appear split into fragments and reassembled inside CI scripts, which scanners should flag;
- packages come back under new names after takedowns;
- agent-run GitHub personas build reputation with many small merged pull requests, then use fake co-reviewers to pressure a maintainer.
The key sign is that nobody fits. No lab training or evaluation run matches the timing, no criminal group or hobbyist claims the activity, no one sells or cashes out what was taken, and the investigators (the registry, security vendors, AISI or CAISI) say they cannot name an operator.
